### SCENARIO 5 — one dataset, two roles, walked in sequence.
###
### A dispatcher sets up a day's work. A technician then does what a technician does: sees their own
### jobs and nobody else's, opens one, tries something their role does not cover, and asks for a job
### that is not theirs. All four answers come from ONE system state, which is what makes them
### comparable — three isolated cases can each be right about a different world.
###
### The four answers are deliberately four DIFFERENT things:
###   the technician's list          200 with a subset      (a rule is a row filter)
###   the technician's own job       200 with the row
###   an operation with no rule      403                    (nothing says who may)
###   somebody else's job            404                    (the row is excluded, not the caller)

### The dispatcher's setup: three jobs, two of them for this technician.
# @name DispatcherCreatesMine1
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8401",
  "title": "Morning call — mine",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "assigned_to": "{{userSpareNorth}}",
  "access_code": "ROLE-1",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

###
# @name DispatcherCreatesMine2
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8402",
  "title": "Afternoon call — mine",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 2,
  "assigned_to": "{{userSpareNorth}}",
  "access_code": "ROLE-2",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The third is somebody else's, and it is the row the 404 below is about
# @name DispatcherCreatesSomebodyElses
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8403",
  "title": "Somebody else's call",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 3,
  "assigned_to": "{{userTechNorth}}",
  "access_code": "ROLE-3",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The dispatcher sees all three, which is the baseline every claim below is measured against.
# @name DispatcherSeesEverything
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?reference=in.(WO-8401,WO-8402,WO-8403)&order=reference.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### 1. The technician's own list: a subset. Not everything, and not nothing.
# @name TechnicianListsTheirDay
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?order=reference.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keySpareNorth}}

### 2. Opening one of their own jobs.
# @name TechnicianOpensTheirJob
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{DispatcherCreatesMine1.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keySpareNorth}}

### ...and working on it. `work_orders.update` admits a technician for a row assigned to them.
# @name TechnicianStartsTheirJob
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{DispatcherCreatesMine1.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keySpareNorth}}
If-Match: {{TechnicianOpensTheirJob.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "in_progress" }

### 3. An operation no rule covers. `regions` declares no delete at all, so nobody may — which is
### why the answer names the operation and not the caller.
# @name TechnicianDeletesARegion
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/regions/{{RegionNorthId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keySpareNorth}}

### And the shape it must be distinguishable from: a configured rule the technician fails, which is
### a 200 with an empty page rather than a refusal.
# @name TechnicianListsCustomers
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers?limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keySpareNorth}}

### 4. Somebody else's job, by id.
# @name TechnicianOpensSomebodyElsesJob
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{DispatcherCreatesSomebodyElses.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keySpareNorth}}

### ...and cannot write it either
# @name TechnicianWritesSomebodyElsesJob
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{DispatcherCreatesSomebodyElses.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keySpareNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "cancelled" }

### THE STATE OF THE WORLD, read by the dispatcher who can see all three: the technician's own job
### moved, and the job that was refused did not.
# @name FinalState
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?reference=in.(WO-8401,WO-8402,WO-8403)&order=reference.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
